      "math_academy_id": "q-340949",
      "knowledge_point_id": "107eaf98-be8c-5fb1-ac4c-948b1e00df0a",
      "knowledge_point": "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Nonlinear Exponent",
      "problem": "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y={e}^{4{x}^{3}}$ at the point where $x=1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u=4{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=12{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (12{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =12{x}^{2}{e}^{u} \\\\  & =12{x}^{2}{e}^{4{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=1$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(12{x}^{2}{e}^{4{x}^{3}})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =12(1)^{2}{e}^{4(1)^{3}} \\\\  & =12{e}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
