      "math_academy_id": "q-48659",
      "knowledge_point_id": "2f4bfd24-dafd-57b3-940a-154a6e0acf3d",
      "knowledge_point": "Differentiating a General Exponential Function Using the Chain Rule",
      "problem": "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y={4}^{7x}$ at the point where $x=\\frac{1}{7}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={4}^{u},\\,u=7x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={4}^{u}ln⁡4,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=7.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={4}^{u}ln⁡4\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =7ln⁡4\\cdot {4}^{u} \\\\  & =7ln⁡4\\cdot {4}^{7x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=\\frac{1}{7}$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=1/7} & =7ln⁡(4)\\cdot {4}^{7(1/7)} \\\\  & =7ln⁡(4)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =28ln⁡(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{1}{7}$ is $28ln⁡(4).$",
