      "problem": "Given that $y=3\\cdot {4}^{{x}^{2}-1},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=3\\cdot {4}^{u},\\,u={x}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=3\\cdot {4}^{u}ln⁡4,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {4}^{u}ln⁡4\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =6x\\cdot {4}^{{x}^{2}-1}ln⁡4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
