      "math_academy_id": "e-727",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a83f86ab-a008-5487-b118-f1495a0fe4d6",
      "knowledge_point": "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Linear Exponent",
      "problem": "Calculate $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = e^{5x +4}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf = e^u, \\qquad u=5x+4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = e^u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =5{e}^{u} \\\\  & =5{e}^{5x+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-729",
      "knowledge_point_id": "107eaf98-be8c-5fb1-ac4c-948b1e00df0a",
      "knowledge_point": "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Nonlinear Exponent",
      "problem": "Given that $y=e^{-x^2},$ calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = e^u, \\qquad u=-x^2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = e^u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =-2x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-2x) \\\\  & =-2x{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-728",
      "knowledge_point_id": "2f4bfd24-dafd-57b3-940a-154a6e0acf3d",
      "knowledge_point": "Differentiating a General Exponential Function Using the Chain Rule",
      "problem": "Given that $y= 3\\cdot 2^{1 - x},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = 3\\cdot 2^u, \\qquad u=1-x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = 3\\cdot 2^u\\ln(2),\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =-1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{u}ln⁡(2)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {2}^{u}ln⁡(2) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {2}^{1-x}ln⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
